    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_title">Leydig cells and testosterone</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_choice">Leydig endocrine output</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_scope">A schematic of the typical adult testis. Shapes, markers, and playback timing are for teaching only and do not show personal anatomy, hormone levels, or measurements.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_location">Between the tubules</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_location_caption">Leydig cells sit in the interstitium, the tissue outside and between the seminiferous tubules, near small blood vessels.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_signal">A signal from the pituitary</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_signal_caption">Luteinizing hormone (LH) travels in the blood from the anterior pituitary and signals to Leydig cells.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_production">Testosterone production</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_production_caption">In response to LH, adult Leydig cells make and release testosterone, their main androgen hormone.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_output">Local support and endocrine output</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_leydig_endocrine_output_caption">Testosterone acts locally to support sperm development in nearby tubules. Some enters the blood and can act elsewhere in the body.</string>
