    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_title">Theca and granulosa cooperation</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_choice">Theca hormone relay</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_scope">A schematic of a typical adult ovarian antral follicle. Layers, markers, and playback timing are for teaching only and do not show personal anatomy, hormone levels, or measurements.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_layer">The vascular theca interna</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_layer_caption">The theca interna lies outside the follicular basal lamina, beneath the theca externa. Small blood vessels run in the theca, not in the granulosa layer.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_source">Theca cells make androgens</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_source_caption">Blood-borne luteinizing hormone (LH) stimulates cells in the theca interna to make androgens.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_handoff">Androgen crosses the boundary</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_handoff_caption">Androgen from the theca crosses the basal lamina into the granulosa layer. The blood vessels remain outside that boundary.</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_conversion">Granulosa cells make estradiol</string>
    <string name="health_atlas_theca_cooperation_conversion_caption">With support from follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), granulosa cells convert theca-derived androgens into estrogens, including estradiol.</string>
